# ${project.name}

The PMD Plugin runs the [PMD](https://pmd.github.io/) static code analyzer and its Copy/Paste Detector (CPD) on your project's source code. It can fail the build on violations or duplicated code, and it can publish both results as reports on your project site.

This version uses PMD ${pmdVersion} and needs Maven ${project.prerequisites.maven} or later running on Java ${javaVersion} or later. To use a newer PMD release, see [Upgrading PMD at Runtime](./examples/upgrading-PMD-at-runtime.html).

The most common tasks are:

- Check the code against the default PMD rules and fail the build on violations. Bind the goal into your build as shown in [Violation Checking](./examples/violationChecking.html), or run it directly:

    ```shell
    mvn compile pmd:check
    ```

- Find duplicated code and fail the build when CPD reports any:

    ```shell
    mvn pmd:cpd-check
    ```

- Add the PMD and CPD reports to the project site by declaring the plugin in the `<reporting>` section (see [Usage](./usage.html)), then run `mvn site`.
- Use your own rules instead of the default ruleset, as described in [Using Rule Sets](./examples/usingRuleSets.html).

Goals Overview
--------------

This plugin has the following goals:

- [pmd:pmd](./pmd-mojo.html) creates a PMD site report based on the rulesets and configuration set in the plugin. It can also generate a pmd output file aside from the site report in any of the following formats: xml, csv or txt.
- [pmd:aggregate-pmd](./aggregate-pmd-mojo.html) creates a PMD site report in an **aggregator** project based on the rulesets and configuration set in the plugin. It can also generate a pmd output file aside from the site report in any of the following formats: xml, csv or txt.
- [pmd:aggregate-pmd-no-fork](./aggregate-pmd-no-fork-mojo.html) creates a PMD site report in an **aggregator** project without forking the `test-compile` phase again.
- [pmd:cpd](./cpd-mojo.html) creates a report for PMD's Copy/Paste Detector (CPD) tool. It can also generate a cpd results file in any of these formats: xml, csv or txt.
- [pmd:aggregate-cpd](./aggregate-cpd-mojo.html) creates a report for PMD's Copy/Paste Detector (CPD) tool in an **aggregator** project. It can also generate a cpd results file in any of these formats: xml, csv or txt.
- [pmd:check](./check-mojo.html) fails the build if there were any PMD violations in the source code. This goal invokes automatically `pmd:pmd` prior to executing itself.
- [pmd:aggregate-pmd-check](./aggregate-pmd-check-mojo.html) fails the build in an **aggregator** project if there were any PMD violations in the source code. This goal invokes automatically `pmd:aggregate-pmd` prior to executing itself.
- [pmd:cpd-check](./cpd-check-mojo.html) fails the build if there were any CPD violations in the source code. This goal invokes automatically `pmd:cpd` prior to executing itself.
- [pmd:aggregate-cpd-check](./aggregate-cpd-check-mojo.html) fails the build in an **aggregator** project if there were any CPD violations in the source code. This goal invokes automatically `pmd:aggregate-cpd` prior to executing itself.

Usage
-----

General instructions on how to use the PMD Plugin can be found on the [usage page](./usage.html). Some more specific use cases are described in the examples given below. If you still have questions, see the [FAQ](./faq.html) or ask on the [user mailing list](./mailing-lists.html).

To report a defect or request a feature, open an issue in the [issue tracker](./issue-management.html) with steps to reproduce, ideally a small demo project. To contribute a fix, see the [source repository](./scm.html) and the [guide to helping with Maven](https://maven.apache.org/guides/development/guide-helping.html).

Upgrading Notes
---------------

#[[### 3.29.0]]#

- Starting with Maven PMD Plugin 3.29.0, the plugin automatically uses the value of `targetJdk` to resolve the Java platform classes via a matching toolchain. For [type resolution](pmd-mojo.html#typeResolution), PMD reads the class files of both dependencies and the Java platform. If the wrong Java platform is selected, this might result in false positive and/or false negative violations. It is therefore important to have a toolchain configured with a version matching targetJdk. See [Target JDK and Toolchains](./examples/targetJdk.html).

#[[### 3.22.0]]#

- Starting with Maven PMD Plugin 3.22.0, the plugin requires PMD version 7.0.0 or higher. PMD 7 logs through SLF4J, which Maven uses for its own logging. Logs from PMD are now always shown and cannot be disabled at runtime after maven has started. The property `showPmdLog` makes no sense anymore and is deprecated now. See [Maven Logging](https://maven.apache.org/maven-logging.html) for how to configure logging. For disabling PMD logs, you'd need to start maven with `MAVEN_OPTS=-Dorg.slf4j.simpleLogger.log.net.sourceforge.pmd=off mvn <goals> `.
- The upgrade from PMD 6 to PMD 7.0.0 is a major version change. If you use the default ruleset from Maven PMD Plugin, then everything should just work. But if you use a custom ruleset, you most likely need to review your ruleset and migrate it to PMD 7. Rules might have been renamed or replaced. See [Detailed Release Notes for PMD 7](https://docs.pmd-code.org/pmd-doc-7.0.0/pmd_release_notes_pmd7.html) and [Migration Guide for PMD 7](https://docs.pmd-code.org/latest/pmd_userdocs_migrating_to_pmd7.html).
- If you currently override the dependency to PMD ([Upgrading PMD at Runtime](./examples/upgrading-PMD-at-runtime.html)) make sure to upgrade PMD as well to 7.0.0 or later when upgrading the Maven PMD Plugin.

